# An overview on metal-related catalysts: metal oxides, nanoporous metals and supported metal nanoparticles on metal organic frameworks and zeolites

Abstract Metal nanoparticles (NPs) supported on porous materials have shown great advantages in many catalytic application fields. Supported metal NPs are receiving extensive attention due to their significant contribution in a wide range of current and future applications, and this is arguably one of the fastest growing research fields. In this review, we highlight various types of metal catalysts that possess great potential in several catalytic reactions. The major focus has been on metal oxides, nanoporous metals and metal NPs supported on metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and zeolites. Special attention has been given to the synthesis strategies and application of the NPs supported on MOFs and zeolites, which are considered highly interesting and rapidly expanding areas in heterogeneous catalysis. Finally, the prospects of these catalysts have been included in the concluding remarks.
Keywords Supported metal catalysts; Porous materials; Nanoporous metals; Heterogeneous catalysis; Zeolites; Metal oxides; Metal–organic frameworks
## 1 Introduction
Over the past few decades, metal nanoparticles (NPs)/metal catalysts have been widely used in heterogeneous catalysis in various industrial applications [1–5]. The metal catalysts may be present in various forms: metal oxides, porous metals and supported metal NPs on porous materials [6–11]. Research on metal catalysts has received considerable attention, and the recent trends suggest that it would continue to increase in the future (Fig. 1). The catalytic efficiency of metal catalysts largely depends on the NP surface along with their size, shape and composition. The supported porous materials may have large surface area, large porosity and a hierarchically ordered pore structure to be used for several applications including heterogeneous catalysis and separation. Porous metals (also called low-weight materials due to their characteristics of low bulk density, large surface area and low thermal conductivity) are a new class of porous materials that have excellent functional and structural properties [12–14]. Porous metal oxides (single- and mixed-metal oxides) with high surface area have been extensively used in various catalytic reactions [15, 16]. The rational design of ordered metal oxides has shown great potential to deliver nextgeneration industrial catalysts with remarkable catalytic performance. The pore size and structural design of the ordered porous metal oxide strongly depend on the type of template molecules used in the synthesis of the materials.
Ordered porous materials are very important in the field of heterogeneous catalysis, and they produce remarkable scientific and industrial impacts. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and zeolites are the two of the most important porous materials being used in heterogeneous catalysis. Metal–organic frameworks are highly porous materials and have widely attracted the attention of material scientists due to their exciting properties and applications. In contrast to other inorganic materials, MOFs are inorganic–organic composite materials constructed from metal ions or clusters and with organic linkers to form 3D coordination networks [17–20]. Mostly, all the transition metals and a vast variety of organic linkers have been applied in the synthesis of metal NPs supported on MOFs $(\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{MOFs})$ ), which have resulted in numerous MOFs frameworks with different structures [21]. Due to the presence of large surface area and tunable porous architecture, MOFs have great capabilities for various applications in heterogeneous catalysis [22]. However, the low thermal and chemical stability of MOFs is the biggest concern with respect to long-term, durable applications.
Unlike the MOFs, zeolites are highly crystalline and stable porous materials [23]. They are known to be an important group of catalytic materials and are highly applicable in various heterogeneous catalytic reactions [24–26]. So far, 230 types of zeolite frameworks are officially recognized by the Structure Commission of the International Zeolite Association (IZA-SC). Metal NPs supported on zeolites $\mathrm{NPs}@$ zeolites) obtained via encapsulation, impregnation and ion-exchange methods have further enriched the category of metal-supported heterogeneous catalysts and their efficient applications in various fields [27].
As discussed above, four types of metal catalysts (metal oxides, nanoporous metals (NPMs), NPs supported on MOFs and NPs supported on zeolites) are very attractive and useful in numerous catalytic reactions. In this review, we aim to provide a detailed study on these catalytic systems. We have focused on each catalytic system separately with respect to their categories and catalytic behaviors. Our intention is to define and present a clear development made toward these catalysts and their growing interest to the society. Particular attention has been given to the advantages of the different metal NPs supported on porous materials (NPs on MOFs and NPs on zeolites) and their heterogeneous catalytic applications. The final section summarizes the main conclusions and the future prospects of these catalysts.
## 2 Metal oxides
With their interesting and unique physicochemical properties, metal oxides are highly regarded as efficient catalysts for several reactions. Here, we will describe various types of metal oxides in the form of single-metal or mixedmetal components as the catalyst itself, along with nanoporous metal oxides as the support for catalytic noble metal.
## 2.1 Single- or mixed-component metal oxides as heterogeneous catalysts
Single-component metal oxides have been well studied for various catalytic applications due to their unique surface chemistry. These metal oxides are inexpensive and readily available compared to precious metals. Considerable attention has been given to the synthesis of several mesoand macroporous single-metal oxides. Here, we specially paid more attention to $\mathrm{CeO}_{2^{-}}$ and $\mathbf{MnO}_{x}$ -type metal oxides. $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ has been widely studied as heterogeneous catalyst due to its unique properties and remarkable applications in various reactions [28, 29]. The catalytic activity of a $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ - based material significantly varies with composition, morphology and surface property. $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ is another extensively explored metal oxide catalyst because of its manifold crystalline phases and various oxidation states [30–32]. It can be obtained in various crystal phases $(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Mn}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{8}.$ ). Toberer and coworkers [33] proposed a practical method for the synthesis of porous $\mathbf{MnO}$ by reducing sintered pellets of $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Later, Ye et al. [34] reported the successful synthesis of the highly porous octahedral-layered $\mathrm{MnO}_{6}$ catalyst and its application for low-temperature CO oxidation.
Single-metal oxides with additional metals that form mixed-component metal oxides can further enhance their catalytic efficiency and stability [35, 36]. Mn with Co- and Ce-based mixed-metal oxides, such as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-MnO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}–\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ catalysts, have shown remarkable performance for gaseous benzene oxidation and diesel soot oxidation, respectively [37, 38]. For both reactions, an enhanced oxidation activity was obtained, which was mainly caused by a very strong synergy between Mn and Ce and Mn and Co (Fig. 2). Chen et al. [39] proposed a novel route for the synthesis of mesoporous, hollow and honeycomb-type ${\bf K}_{x}{\bf M}{\bf n}{\bf O}_{2}$ nanospheres in which the alignment of the ${\mathrm{K}}_{x}{\mathrm{MnO}}_{2}$ nanospheres was determined by the typical ratio of $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ to oleic acid. At ambient temperature, ${\mathrm{K}}_{x}{\mathrm{MnO}}_{2}$ nanospheres showed better catalytic efficiency toward oxidative formaldehyde (HCHO) conversion as compared to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ powders and Mnmodified $\mathrm{Al}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}–\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . That study also compared the morphology effect on the catalytic performance of honeycomb $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ and hollow $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ nanospheres and concluded that the hollow $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ nanosphere achieved higher activity for HCHO oxidation, irrespective of having two times less surface area than the honeycomb $\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ structure. Therefore, it can be concluded that the surface area is the key and decisive factor for higher catalytic performance.
## 2.2 Nanoporous metal oxides as support for noblemetal NPs
Meso-/macroporous metal oxides have been extensively applied as supporting materials for Au, Pd, Pt and Ag NPs and are well known as efficient catalysts for various catalytic reactions. Previous works have proved that uniform porous metal oxides are highly suitable supports for various noble metals due to the so-called strong metal–support interaction (SMSI). Noble metals supported on three-dimensionally ordered meso-/macroporous metal oxides (3DOM) have been successfully obtained via different synthesis methods. Wei et al. [40, 41] synthesized the 3DOM $\mathrm{LaFeO}_{3}$ -supported gold NP catalyst via the in situ colloidal crystal templating method; the synthesized material was found to be highly applicable in diesel soot oxidation. For synthesis, the Au NPs were dispersed on polymethyl methacrylate (PMMA) by modified emulsifierfree polymerization, followed by the injection of the perovskite precursors into the PMMA microspheres. Finally, Au NPs containing $\mathrm{LaFeO}_{3}$ on the surface of the 3DOM were obtained after removal of the organic contents via calcination (Fig. 3a, b). Additionally, $\mathrm{La_{0.6}S r_{0.4}M n O_{3}}$ (LSMO) containing $3.4~\mathrm{wt}\%-8.0~\mathrm{wt}\%$ Au NPs was also synthesized with PMMA (template) and gas-assisted polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) reduction [42]. A comparative catalytic performance for CO oxidation was studied with respect to the Au/3DOM LSMO material and the bulk LSMO (Fig. 3c–e). The catalytic efficiency of the former was observed to be much higher than that of the bulk LSMO using the same amounts of Au NPs, which was attributed to the difference in the rate of dispersion of Au
NPs and their higher degree of dispersion with the 3DOM LSMO than with the bulk LSMO, giving higher surface area and hence the higher activity.
## 3 Nanoporous metals (NPMs)
NPMs have had wider applications in catalysis in the optical and sensing sectors due to their larger surface area and high conductivity. Recent developments regarding their synthesis and applicability have created an exciting platform and highly active research field for material scientists aiming at the new class of porous materials.
## 3.1 Synthesis of NPMs
Erlebacher and coworkers developed the first bimodal nanoporous metal in the form of thin Au foil [43]. Later, Nyce et al. [44] obtained 3D hierarchical nanoporous Au by the combined process of templating and dealloying. The hierarchical porous and low-dimensional materials were found to be more resistant to induced cracking than the homogeneous bulk materials [45]. Moreover, templating was also achieved via electrodeposition of metals into defined areas through masking. By this method, Au–Ag nanowires and Pt–Co nanowires were synthesized by electroplating the alloy into the porous template molecules. Subsequently, the nanowires were stripped out from the porous template and dispersed onto the substrate and dealloyed to obtain the nanoporous wires [46, 47]. The conductive substrate was also obtained via vapor deposition of an Au layer [48]. The fabrication of uniform 2D nanoporous Au opal film was achieved via electrodeposition of the $\mathbf{Au-Ag}$ alloy onto a silica template. Finally, a nanoporous Au opal architecture was obtained after removal of the silica template by hydrofluoric acid etching and dealloying in nitric acid [49].
Dealloying is the key method for synthesis of various NPMs. The dealloying method has been widely applied for the synthesis of various nanoporous metallic structures including Pd, Cu, Ni and Pt [50–53]. Au is a particularly studied NPM due to its greater stability and facile dealloying route to control the nanoscale morphology. The scale of the typically dealloyed nanoporous Au is in the
$40{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ range. However, this can be significantly changed by the alloy composition or by altering the dealloying conditions to further reduce the ligament size [54, 55]. In another attempt, a hierarchical nanoporous Pd was synthesized via dealloying of Pd–Al by spark plasma sintering method. A porous structure was obtained in the heterogeneous regions due to the difference in activities for the ${\mathrm{PdAl}}_{3}$ and Al phases. A uniform nanoporous Pd was also synthesized by the dealloying of Pd–Ni–P metallic glass (Fig. 4a, b) [56]. Cox and Dunand [57] obtained a micro-/macroporous Ag–Au structure by mixing salt and metal powder. First, an aqueous salt solution was obtained by dissolving the desired salt in water, which generated block-shaped macropores. The micropores were created from expansion of the water vapor trapped inside the powder. The sintering process alloyed the Au and Ag powders, and finally, and the nanoporosity was obtained by dealloying in nitric acid (Fig. 4c, d).
## 3.2 Catalytic applications of NPMs
Raney nickel (developed in 1926) is being used for a wide range of significant hydrogenation reactions [58]. Raney copper is another NPM, which is mainly used in the water gas shift reaction [59]. The improved methanol oxidation efficiency of nanoporous Pt over its polycrystalline Pt counterpart suggested that the effect of structure of the materials plays a crucial role in catalytic performance [60]. The uniform nanoporous Pd is an effective catalyst, widely used in alcohol oxidation [61]. The nanoporous $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}A u}$ alloy is a very suitable catalyst for formic acid oxidation and has superior activity than $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Fig. 5a, b) [62]. Moreover, studies have shown that nanoporous gold with a particle size between 12 and $30~\mathrm{nm}$ can be very effective and highly valuable for methanol and CO oxidation and oxygen/hydrogen peroxide reduction [63–65]. The significance of the material structure is further enhanced by the recent results obtained on the application of nanoporous Au catalysts in different forms, which indicated that the coating of NPMs provides an advantage for better catalytic activity and selectivity of several catalysts. Pt-coated nanoporous Au has shown high stability and improved catalytic efficiency for methanol reduction than commercial $\mathrm{Pt/Ru}$ catalysts [66]. A further study showed that a higher Pt content resulted in an enhanced catalytic performance, which was attributed to the presence of extra Pt sites [67].
The applicability of NPMs has been further extended by their successful utilization in energy storage devices, including electrodes and supercapacitors. Chen and his coworkers achieved a hierarchical nanoporous Au electrode and applied it in a lithium-ion (Li-ion) battery [68]. The hierarchical nanoporous structure increased its capacity and durability, even at low overpotentials, compared to its non-hierarchical counterparts (Fig. 5c, d). Composite NPMs have also been used to increase the overall efficiency of batteries, which were later used for commercial purposes [69, 70]. For example, nanoporous tin–gold (Sn– Au) composite, obtained via the deposition of tin on nanoporous gold, has been efficiently used in lithium-ion batteries [71]. Additionally, coated nanoporous Cu was also applied in Li-ion batteries [72].
## 4 Metal NPs supported on metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are considered one of the best supporting materials for several metal NPs due to their large surface area and high surface energy, which enable good accessibility of the NPs in the catalysis. They are highly tunable and can be constructed in different structures and pore sizes with various metals and organic ligands. These properties permit the easy selection of suitable MOF materials depending on the size of the desired metal NPs. Until now, several synthesis methods have been employed for the incorporation of different NPs, such as Pd, Pt, Au, Ag, Ru, Cu and Ni, into various MOFs [73].
## 4.1 Synthesis of NPs supported on MOFs
Impregnation is the most commonly used approach for the encapsulation of NPs in MOFs. It comprises two steps. Firstly, the porous support is immersed in a metal precursor solution. Subsequently, after removal of the solvent, the impregnated metal ions are reduced by $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ to obtain the metallic NPs in the second step. Encapsulation of metal NPs into the MOFs can also be achieved by grinding the metallic precursors with the MOF materials in the absence of any solvent [74, 75]. Unlike liquid-phase impregnation and solid-phase grinding, gas-phase impregnation involves easy encapsulation of the volatile organometallic precursors in gas phase into the MOFs by virtue of their reduction under different conditions [76]. Notably, this is a solvent-free method and allows high loading of volatile precursors into the MOF. The gas-phase impregnation method can be divided into two categories, chemical vapor deposition (CVD) and atomic layer deposition (ALD). In CVD, the metallic precursors are encapsulated into the MOF pores by a sublimation process (Fig. 6a) [77]. This method has been extensively applied to encapsulate various NPs in MOFs such as Pd, Ni, Au and bimetallic NPs such as FePt, PdPt and RuPt (Fig. 6b) [78, 79]. However, in order to control the amount of metals for loading, ALD is considered the one of the most successful methods [80]. This method is more advanced and unique as the exposure of the supporting materials to the vapor can be completed layer by layer with a uniform atomic thickness [81].
## 4.2 Catalytic applications of NPs supported on MOFs
Li et al. reported the remarkable performance of Pt NPs incorporated in MIL-101 $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}.101)}\end{array}$ for the hydrogenation of nitrobenzene in ethanol. The hydrogenation of nitrobenzene was observed to be very fast, and it achieved nearly complete conversion with a turnover frequency (TOF) value of $18,053~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under $4.053\times10^{6}\mathrm{Pa}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and room temperature [82]. $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ also demonstrated efficient activity and selectivity for the hydrogenation of substituted benzaldehyde under ambient conditions. Up to $98\%$ conversion of the substituted benzaldehyde and $100\%$ selectivity were achieved within $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ under $4.053\times10^{6}~\mathrm{P}\mathrm{:}$ a of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and only $0.048\%$ of $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ loading. In a similar manner to the hydrogenation of nitrobenzene, a significant activity of $\mathsf{N P s@M O F}$ was obtained for the selective hydrogenation of olefins. Henschel et al. [83] carried out a comparative size selective hydrogenation of olefins on $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ , ${\mathrm{Pd}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}5$ and $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{C}$ . They observed that $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ performed a higher activity for hydrogenation of styrene ( $100\%$ conversion of styrene to ethyl benzene within $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under only $1.013\times10^{5}$ Pa of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{\dot{\Psi}}}\end{array}$ ) than ${\mathrm{Pd}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}5$ and Pd/C (Fig. 7a).
Another interesting report by Na et al. [84] on the growth of nanocrystalline metal–organic frameworks (nMOFs) around metal nanocrystals (NCs) is highly useful in controlling the chemistry and metric of metal NCs. They observed that Pt NPs-encapsulated nMOF nanocrystals $({\mathrm{Pt}}\subset{\mathrm{nMOF}}$ are highly active in the gas-phase hydrogenative conversion of methylcyclopentane (MCP) and resulted in unusual product selectivity (Fig. 7b–d). They encapsulated $\mathrm{Pt}$ NPs in nUiO-66 MOF, and the resultant $\mathsf{P t}\subset$ nUiO-66 showed selectivity toward $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ -cyclic hydrocarbons such as cyclohexane and benzene, which occurred with $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ lower temperature than the standard reaction. In addition, the pore size effect in the MOFs was also observed to be crucial; the Pt NPs encapsulated in nMOF-801 having small pore $(\mathrm{Pt}\subset\mathrm{nMOF}–801)$ ) did not produce the same products, whereas the $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ NPs encapsulated in nUiO-67 having larger pore $\mathsf{P t}\subset$ nUiO-67 catalyst yielded the same products but with different selectivities. Additionally, from the commercial perspective and in terms of cost and efficiency, a single-step selective
