hydrogenation of phenol in gas and aqueous phase with $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ , Ni, Pd and Rh catalysts was observed to be more effective than the two-step high-temperature reactions. $\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{MOF}$ showed excellent reactivity for the selective hydrogenation of phenol. Liu et al. [85] reported that $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ exhibited high catalytic activity with approximately $100\%$ conversion of phenol and $99\%$ selectivity for cyclohexanone due to the formation of $2\mathrm{-}3~\mathrm{{nm}}$ chromium $(\mathbf{Cr})$ NPs inside the MIL-101 pores and the availability of the Lewis acidic sites, which inhibited further hydrogenation of cyclohexanone.
Dehydrogenation is another important reaction that involves the removal of molecular hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ from organic compounds in the presence of catalysts. This is a highly significant phenomenon because $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is known as a new-generation alternative energy. Up to now, $\mathbf{Rh}/\mathbf{CeO}_{2}$ has been reported as the most suitable catalyst for dehydrogenation from the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ at room temperature with a TOF of $2010\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ [86]. The MOF is considered to be one of the excellent hosts for mono-, bi-, and trimetallic NPs. $\mathsf{N P s@M O F}$ exhibited good catalytic efficiency for dehydrogenation reactions of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{BH}_{3}$ , formic acid and phenol derivatives [87, 88]. An enhanced activity was obtained due to the formation of ultrafine $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ NPs ( $\mathrm{~\sim~}0.2~\mathrm{nm})$ dispersed inside the pores of MIL-101. $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ is a more suitable catalyst than the industrially used Rh catalyst in terms of stability and recyclability (Fig. 8a, b) [89]. $\mathtt{R u C u C o@M I L-101}$ is another important $\mathbf{NPs}@\mathbf{MOF}$ trimetallic nanocatalyst applied for the hydrolysis of ammonia borane (AB) with a TOF of $241.2\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ (Fig. 8c, d) [90]. From the available literature, it can be understood that the development of $\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{MOF}$ for the dehydrogenation of chemical compounds is still in progress. Significant catalytic efficiencies, especially the TOFs of $\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{MOF}$ , were found in a far shorter period of time than with other nanocatalysts. In several cases, the nature of the MOF, size of NPs and their interaction and synergistic effect are some of the key factors responsible for controlling their catalytic activity [91].
${\mathrm{NP}}@{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ have also attracted many researchers to study their applicability in Suzuki–Miyaura carbon–carbon (C–C) coupling reactions. So far, only Pd NPs have been successfully applied in Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reactions [92, 93]. Yuan et al. [94] first reported the Suzuki coupling reaction of aryl chlorides by using the $\mathbf{Pd}@\mathbf{MIL-}101$ catalyst and observed the remarkable performance of this catalyst in $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-C}$ coupling reactions. They claimed that the large surface area, pore size and Lewis acidity are the decisive factors to favor the adsorption of the substrate, which resulted in enhanced overall catalytic activity. However, decomposition of the MOF substrate was also observed due to the employed base. Further studies were carried out with other NPs such as nickel $(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MOF})$ , and it was proved that Ni-based catalysts are versatile and more capable in $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ coupling, which is more challenging in the presence of Pd-based catalysts [95]. Moreover, Ni is also cost-effective (it has lower cost) and it is more abundant than Pd.
## 5 Metal nanoparticles supported on zeolites
Zeolites have been widely used as ideal supporting materials to metal NPs because of their highly stable porous crystalline structure and low synthesis costs. Recent advancements in synthesis strategies and modifications of various zeolite structures have enhanced their applicability in various heterogeneous catalytic reactions. Because of the intercrystalline and intracrystalline mesopore systems, zeolites are considered to be highly useful supporting materials for various metals, both in the microporous cavities and in the mesoporous channels of the zeolitic frameworks. Moreover, the presence of both Brønsted and Lewis acid sites (useful in heterogeneous catalysis) increases their importance as useful supporting materials for the encapsulation, impregnation and ion exchange of different metals/metal nanoparticles. Here, we will focus on the significant progress achieved in the design, synthesis and application of zeolite-containing metal NPs in various fields.
## 5.1 Synthesis of metal NPs supported on zeolites
Till the recent years, various methods have been applied for the synthesis of various metal NPs supported on zeolites. Among them, wet impregnation (containing the solutions of metal salts) is one of the most suitable and widely applied methods to introduce various metals into different zeolite frameworks [96]. However, a major drawback with impregnation is that it leads to the disordered/uneven distribution of the metals on the supporting materials. In contrast to wet impregnation, ion exchange is considered to be a facile and more convenient method for the synthesis of transition metal cation-exchanged zeolite composites [97]. Ion exchange is carried out in an aqueous medium with continuous treatment of a targeted metal followed by washing. Chemical vapor deposition (CVD) is another method that permits the preparation of well-defined atomic-scale catalysts via a stoichiometric reaction between Brønsted acid sites and the metal precursor, resulting in the selective deposition of metal cations. Some significant contributions have been made for the deposition of various metals on a zeolite support via this method by using their respective precursors, for example, Ni with $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5})_{2})$ , Sn with $\mathrm{(SnCl_{4})}$ and Ga with $\left(\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{3}\right)$ [98–100]. Titanium-containing zeolites named as Ti-beta and MFI type (TS-1) are known to be important metal-substituted zeolite-based catalysts, synthesized by the incorporation of Ti atoms into the silicate framework using titanium alkoxide sources [101]. TS1 is a highly efficient catalyst in the selective epoxidation of olefins and hydroxylation of phenolics. Additionally, Snbeta zeolite was also obtained by the controlled top-down synthesis approach and is considered to be an efficient catalyst for the conversion of various biomass-derived chemicals to value-added products [102].
In another approach, the encapsulation of $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles inside the zeolite was achieved via recrystallization of the zeolitic frameworks in the presence of a metal precursor and structure direction agent (SDA). Corma and coworkers successfully encapsulated highly stable subnanometric platinum during the transformation of a 2D (MWW framework) to 3D (MCM-22) zeolite structure in the presence of hexadecyltrimethyl ammonium hydroxide surfactant (CTMAOH) [103]. In this approach, they mixed the platinum precursor (platinum(II) acetylacetonate) in dimethylformamide (DMF) solvent. This mixture was added to the aqueous suspension of the lamellar precursor (2D MWW framework) containing solutions of CTMAOH and tetrapropylammonium hydroxide (TPAOH). The resultant mixture was further heated and synthesized at predetermined conditions, and the transformed and a highly stable 3D $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{MCM-}22$ subnanometric encapsulated structure was obtained after calcination (Fig. 9). Additionally, some significant efforts were also made for the encapsulation of Pt and Au NPs inside silicalite-1 by the impregnation method followed by recrystallization in the presence of TPAOH solution [104, 105].
## 5.2 Catalytic applications
Metal NPs supported on zeolites have been widely applied in various heterogeneous catalytic reactions. The controlled substitution of Si atoms with tetrahedrally coordinated Ti, Sn and $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms in zeolite frameworks generates Lewis acid sites (LA), which are greatly useful in biomass conversion reactions and have shown greater efficiency over other zeolites containing Brønsted acid sites (BA) only [106]. Sn-beta zeolite contains both LA and BA sites and is effectively applied as heterogeneous catalyst for the multistep conversion of 1,3-dihydroxyacetone into ethyl lactate [107]. In multistep catalysis, the BA sites (at the aluminum tetrahedra) determine the dehydration of dihydroxyacetone to pyruvic aldehyde, and the LA sites (at the Sn tetrahedral) control the conversion of pyruvic aldehyde to ethyl lactate. Moreover, zeolites with noble-metal NPs exhibit remarkable catalytic efficiency due to the shape selectivity of the zeolites and activity of the noble metals. For example, $\mathrm{Au}/$ silicalite-1 (prepared by the encapsulation of dispersed Au NPs) achieved excellent stability and a catalytic efficiency of $50\%$ in bioethanol conversion with $98\%$ acetaldehyde selectivity (Fig. 10a, b) [108]. Na et al. [109] reported the effect of the acidic properties of Pt NPs supported on mesoporous BEA zeolites for the conversion of methylcyclopentane (MCP). They observed a significant impact of the acidic properties of zeolite on product selectivity and catalytic efficiency for the hydrogenative isomerization of methylcyclopentane and demonstrated that the selectivities of cyclohexane and benzene can be systematically altered according to the different mesoporous BEA zeolites. Their study concluded that the formation of cyclohexane and benzene depends on the Br¨onsted/Lewis acid (B/L) ratio in the zeolite frameworks. A higher $\mathrm{\mathbf{B}/L}$ ratio in the zeolite favored benzene formation, whereas a catalyst containing a comparatively lower B/L ratio enhanced the formation of cyclohexane (Fig. 10c, d).
Metal NPs supported on zeolite materials have also been applied as an efficient catalyst for the production of hydrogen and methanol, and in electrodes [110, 111]. Wang et al. [112] proposed a simple approach for the encapsulation of ultra-small Pd within nanosized silicalite1 and applied it for efficient $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production from the decomposition of formic acid under mild conditions. Because of the captivity of Pd clusters inside the silicalite-1 frameworks, the synthesized composite material showed higher thermal stability and remarkable reusability. Metal– zeolite composite materials have also shown great applicability for the production of methanol. For example, in an anaerobic reaction, Cu-exchanged mordenite was applied for the efficient and highly selective conversion of methane to methanol [113]. Moreover, the $\mathrm{CeO_{2}/Z S M-5}$ composite catalyst was successfully applied as a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) for methanol oxidation, and it exhibited three times higher performance than a commercial catalyst $20\%$ Pt/C) (Fig. 11) [114]. Metal–zeolite composite materials have also been applied for capturing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . For example, Cu-exchanged SSZ-13 was efficiently applied for the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ [115]. Comparatively, small porous zeolites with isoreticular structure were observed to be more suitable for excellent $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selectivity. NaTEAZSM-25 exhibited fast $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity and attained adsorption equilibrium within $2~\mathrm{min}$ , and it remained unchanged over 100 cycles [116].
## 6 Conclusion and future prospects
In this review, we have summarized and discussed developments for the synthesis and application of various types of metal catalysts (metal oxides, porous metals and metal
NPs supported on MOFs and zeolites). Here, we have briefly described the single- and mixed-component metal oxides and their use as supporting materials for other metals in order to generate an ordered porous structure and to improve the catalytic efficiency for various reactions. Various studies have proved that the ordered, porous metal oxides enhance the catalytic efficiency, selectivity and stability of various reactions. The increasing research interest in the synthesis of various ordered and porous metal oxide nanostructures will probably result in new applications in heterogeneous catalysis and other fields. The synthesis and applications of some important NPMs have also been presented. The synthesis of NPMs is mainly achieved via templating and dealloying methods. Further studies on hierarchical NPMs need to focus on how to control the pore sizes and the surface/interface composition, and to improve their visibility for potential applications in various fields.
As mentioned earlier, this review has explored in particular the synthesis and application of metal NPs supported on MOFs and zeolites. Here, we have highlighted the synthesis strategies and applications of various NPs supported on MOFs and zeolites. MOFs have been a powerful support material to host various NPs. The enhanced catalytic activity of NPs supported or encapsulated on MOFs clearly justifies why they are often treated as superior supports. This is because the MOFs contain large surface areas, tunable pore sizes and unique organic linkers that interact with the reactants by non-covalent interactions, thus mitigating the possible leaching of NPs during the catalytic reactions. In spite of the significant applications of $\mathrm{NPs}@\mathrm{MOFs}$ in various heterogeneous catalysis, there are some concerns that still need to be addressed. The stability of MOFs is one of the most important and critical issues, which limits aspects as their durability in practical applications. More insights on the stability and lifetime of the NPs supported on MOFs materials are necessary in future studies. Stability of NPs supported on MOFs can be improved through the adjustment in the synthesis conditions: (1) by using MIX-MOF (incorporation of two different organic linker molecules), and (2) by the utilization of organic solvents in place of water at low temperatures under vacuum. The use of different organic linkers can facilitate the synthesis of tunable functional materials, which can efficiently enhance the thermal stability and the number of active sites. Additionally, more attention is required toward the functionalization of MOFs with inorganic species, such as zeolite imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), an important section of MOFs with significant chemical stability. Moreover, the future studies should also focus on altering the synthesis parameters including the metal NPs (bimetallic and trimetallic alloys), organic linkers, type of solvents, etc. In the final section, we have focused on different synthesis methods and applications of metal NPs supported on zeolite. In contrast to the MOFs, zeolites are highly crystalline and have a stable porous structure, which makes them durable and recyclable heterogeneous catalysts useful in several industrial applications. A thorough description was provided on the synthesis and characterization of well-defined NPs supported on zeolites via encapsulation, impregnation and ion-exchange methods. However, further understanding on stable and selective zeolite-based catalysts is still desirable and can be achieved with the combination of computational modeling and advanced in situ spectroscopy techniques.
