Bhupendra Kumar Singh received his Ph.D. degree in inorganic chemistry from School of Studies in Chemistry, Jiwaji University, Gwalior, Madhya Pradesh, in India, in November 2010. He has worked as a postdoctoral researcher at the Institut de Physique Nucle´- aire (IPN), University of ParisSud, Orsay, in France; School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai in China; and at the Department of
Chemistry, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan in South Korea. Currently, he is working as a postdoctoral researcher at the Department of Chemistry, Chonnam National University, Gwangju, in South Korea. His research interests include ‘‘synthesis of different types of micro- and mesoporous zeolites, characterization and their application in various heterogeneous catalytic reactions.’’
Sunwoo Lee received his Ph.D. degree in organic chemistry from the Department of Chemistry, Pohang University of Science and Technology (POSTECH) in South Korea in August 1999 under the supervision of Prof. Jaiwook Park. He did his postdoctoral studies in the Department of Chemistry at Yale University, USA between October 1999 and August 2001 with Professor John F. Hartwig. He worked at LG Chem. as a senior researcher between August 2001
and February 2004. As of February 2004, he began his current position as a professor in the Department of Chemistry at Chonnam National University in South Korea. The main focus of his work is the development of catalytic transformations and their applications.
Kyungsu Na received his Ph.D. degree in physical chemistry from the Department of Chemistry, Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST) in South Korea in February 2011 under the supervision of Prof. Ryong Ryoo. He has worked as a postdoctoral researcher at KAIST and Institute for Basic Sciences (IBS) in South Korea after his graduation and then continued his postdoctoral research at University of California, Berkeley, USA,
between November 2012 and February 2015. As of March 2015, he has been working as an assistant professor in the Department of Chemistry at Chonnam National University in South Korea. He is currently working as an associate professor as of October 2018, and his major research interests include design of various nanostructured catalysts for chemical conversions of C1 resources to value-added products via energy-efficient catalytic processes.
